{{also|Nephew}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|nevew}}, {{m|enm|neveu|t=nephew, grandson}}, from {{der|en|fro|neveu}}, from {{der|en|la|nepos|nepos, nepōtem}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*nepōts|t=nephew, grandson}}, whence also {{cog|fr|neveu}}, {{cog|it|nipote}}. Displaced or absorbed the inherited {{m+|en|neve|t=nephew, grandson, male cousin}}, from {{cog|enm|neve}}, from {{cog|ang|nefa}}, from {{cog|gmw-pro|*nefō}}, from {{cog|gem-pro|*nefô|t=nephew, grandson}}, whence {{cog|nl|neef}}, {{cog|de|Neffe}}. All ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*népōts|t=grandchild, sister's son}}. Cognate with {{cog|sh|nećak}}, {{cog|ga|nia}}, {{cog|fa|نوه|tr=nave}}.

Spelt with ''-ph-'' by readaptation to {{m+|la||nepos}} since the 15th century, which later triggered the {{spelling pronunciation|en|nocap=1}} with {{IPAchar|/f/}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|nĕfʹyo͞o}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈnɛf.ju/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnɛv.ju/|a=RP,dated}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-nephew.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q15224724}}A son of one's [[sibling]], [[brother-in-law]], or [[sister-in-law]]; either a [[son]] of one's [[brother]] ([[fraternal nephew]]) or a son of one's [[sister]] ([[sororal nephew]]).
#: {{syn|en|neve|q1=obsolete}}
#: {{hyper|en|nephling|nibling}}
#: {{hypo|en|fraternal nephew|sororal nephew}}
#: {{cot|en|niece|nift}}
# A son of one's cousin or cousin-in-law
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A son of one's [[child]].
#: {{syn|en|grandson}}
#* {{RQ:Ovid Golding Metamorphosis|book=1|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A08649.0001.001|passage=Hir father many a time and oft would say my daughter deere,<br>Of '''Nephewes''' thou my debtour art, their Graundsires heart to chéere.}}

